            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Picks at random from a list - numbers, words, rows of a matrix - one at a time or several at once, every item equally likely or weighted.\n\nUse it to choose the next note of a scale, a word to speak, a pose to move to, a clip to play, a direction to turn; with weights, to make some choices common and others rare; without repeats, to deal from a deck - every item once before any comes again.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.random.choice <n>\nDefault: nothing to choose from until a list arrives at from. With n, it chooses from\n0 to n-1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.random.choice 12\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes a choice and sends it.\n\nfrom:\nThe list (or array) to choose from, held. For a 2-D array, whole rows are chosen.\n\ncount (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many to pick. 1 (the default) sends the item itself; more sends them as a list.\n\nweights:\nHow likely each item is, held: one non-negative number per item. They need not add up to 1; 3 1 makes the first item three times as likely as the second. Without weights all are equally likely.\n\nrepeats:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), an item can be picked again within one count. Off, each item at most once per count - like dealing cards (count is then at most the number of items).\n\nseed:\nAn option, under the node. -1 (the default) draws different numbers every time the patch opens. Any other number starts the same sequence every time - the same \"random\" piece on every run, which is what you want for rehearsal, comparison and debugging.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nchosen:\nThe pick, or the list of picks.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: a word from do re mi fa sol, twice a second, each equally likely.\n\nBottom: 16 picks from 0 and 1, weighted 9 to 1: mostly 0s, a 1 now and then - about one in ten.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.random.permutation shuffles a whole list. np.random.integers picks whole numbers in a range. np.take picks by position.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1100,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Picks at random from a list - numbers, words, rows of a matrix - one at a time or several at once, every item equally likely or weighted.\n\nUse it to choose the next note of a scale, a word to speak, a pose to move to, a clip to play, a direction to turn; with weights, to make some choices common and others rare; without repeats, to deal from a deck - every item once before any comes again.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.random.choice <n>\nDefault: nothing to choose from until a list arrives at from. With n, it chooses from\n0 to n-1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.random.choice 12\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes a choice and sends it.\n\nfrom:\nThe list (or array) to choose from, held. For a 2-D array, whole rows are chosen.\n\ncount (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many to pick. 1 (the default) sends the item itself; more sends them as a list.\n\nweights:\nHow likely each item is, held: one non-negative number per item. They need not add up to 1; 3 1 makes the first item three times as likely as the second. Without weights all are equally likely.\n\nrepeats:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), an item can be picked again within one count. Off, each item at most once per count - like dealing cards (count is then at most the number of items).\n\nseed:\nAn option, under the node. -1 (the default) draws different numbers every time the patch opens. Any other number starts the same sequence every time - the same \"random\" piece on every run, which is what you want for rehearsal, comparison and debugging.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nchosen:\nThe pick, or the list of picks.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: a word from do re mi fa sol, twice a second, each equally likely.\n\nBottom: 16 picks from 0 and 1, weighted 9 to 1: mostly 0s, a 1 now and then - about one in ten.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.random.permutation shuffles a whole list. np.random.integers picks whole numbers in a range. np.take picks by position."
        },
